WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.020
It's tougher than any man-made material

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:07.439
It's a combination of strength and elasticity.

00:00:07.439 --> 00:00:10.150
No other material can actually combine these
two features.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.030
Either material is strong or it's elastic.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:14.820
Spider silk is both.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.400
Five times stronger than steel, spider silk
has been tantalizing

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.860
the human imagination for decades.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.620
You want to be Spider Man? 
You have to make fibers.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:27.620
But we can’t just farm the silk , because
spiders eat each other,

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.460
so scientists have pursued other routes.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.060
A DNA sequencing breakthrough in Germany means

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.739
manufacturing spider silk fiber is
no longer pure fantasy.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:41.480
The next step is really producing tons of
the material.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.360
In all the offices we have spiders and they're
like pets.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.680
We like to have them around. 
We think they're beautiful.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.620
If eight-legged co-workers 
make your skin crawl,

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.240
then the biomaterials lab at Bayreuth
University probably isn’t for you.

00:01:03.249 --> 00:01:08.110
It’s here that Professor Thomas Scheibel
and his team study natural substances like

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:12.670
spider silk in the hope of turning them into
super materials.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:17.750
Spider silk has for a lot of material scientists
been the holy grail for decades.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:24.729
If we take a thumb thick spider fiber, we
could actually get a jumbo jet holding up

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:26.799
in air without tearing it apart.

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:32.579
It can withstand hundreds of tons of load
in full speed without breaking.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.980
The best silk producers are orb-weaving spiders.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.500
They have seven different types of silk
with different properties.

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:46.490
We find dramatic differences there concerning
mechanics but also concerning other properties.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.150
Wanting to harness these characteristics led
to Scheibel’s breakthrough in genetically

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:53.240
modifying bacteria to produce
spider silk proteins.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.660
This patented technology formed the basis
of AMSilk, the company Scheibel founded with

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.140
a postdoc from his research lab, Lin Roemer.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:05.980
The bacteria we are using as working horse
are very simple organisms.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.340
They only try to grow. And we exploit that,

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.600
we're feeding them, we give them
perfect conditions to live

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.540
and they multiply and multiply.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.340
And afterwards we change
their way of living,

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:21.620
we give a trigger so 
that they stop proliferating

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.140
and they start producing our protein.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:28.830
After purification, we have the protein in
form of a white powder.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:33.320
AMSilk takes the protein powder, and spins
it into a fiber.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.200
So what you can see here is our spinning machine

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.190
where we produce our Biosteel fiber,

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.840
which is then transferred to the yarner,

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.320
who can make the yarn
and later the fabric

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.140
out of the material.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:52.220
The material is a vegan and biodegradeable
synthetic biopolymer.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.260
Rather than use the BioSteel
material in their own product,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.640
AMSilk wants to produce it for

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.090
established brands, who have expertise.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.820
Adidas used it to create a concept shoe

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:04.820
and Omega made a special edition watch strap.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.540
Airbus are also exploring how
it can be used in aviation.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.460
They know their product.
We develop together with them,

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.040
the product has much higher quality

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.400
in comparison to what we could do on our own.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.600
The company has raised millions in venture
capital, but producing enough of the material

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.120
is the biggest challenge.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.060
As it is very thin you need a lot of
the fibre, in a normal sports shoe

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.840
there are more than 170 
kilometres of this fiber,

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.100
processed into the yarn 
and later the fabric of the shoe

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:42.160
The company says it has made hundreds of
thousands of kilometres of fibre so far

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.800
but scaling it up further will take time and money.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.160
But like the orb-weaving spider
which uses different silks

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.720
for different parts of the web, AMSilk is putting

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.200
the spider silk proteins
to use elsewhere.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:57.340
In the beginning we had this great innovation.
We had thousands of opportunities

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.220
how to use that for industry.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.980
This was a beautiful problem.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.500
You can use it for different applications.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.500
Everyone had the experience when
he goes to the basement

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.420
that there might be a spider's web.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.100
If it's an old house, it might be there for
hundreds of year and it's not degraded.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.700
The question is why?

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:23.760
Microbes have a big problem 
sticking to the web.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.100
If you're lost in the woods, get a web. You
can use it as a wound coverage device, you

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:33.140
can wrap your food. You can actually prevent
your food for rotting for a few days.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:38.410
The material is a proteinaceous materials.
We are all made out of proteins and our body

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:44.460
responds very good to the protein and that's
very suitable for a lot of medical devices.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:50.320
We probably have more than 80 different variants
of silk molecules that we can play with.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.820
Some of them are very well suited for tissue
regeneration,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.860
some of them are absolutely the opposite.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:02.130
We're really just at the beginning into understanding
how this material has evolved to actually

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.230
feature different kinds of applications.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.070
That's exactly what we want.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:10.980
AMSilk is building up production facilities
to make its material at mass scale. It's hoping

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:15.480
to capitalize on the product’s broad potential
and take a lead in an industry harnessing

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.820
the best attributes of natural substances.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:22.260
We're living in a time where materials
made from proteins

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:24.400
will become more important every day.

